VERDICT: CHANGES REQUESTED

The persistence, schema, and `Owner.getMembershipLevel()` refactor (early-returns → assign-then-cap) are clean and idiomatic. The problem is concentrated in `CapMembershipLevel`.

**1. `CapMembershipLevel` has a hidden second responsibility — it mutates other owners' `householdSize`.**

```java
for (Owner member : members) {
    member.setHouseholdSize(members.size());   // <-- not this function's job
    maxLevel = Math.max(maxLevel, member.getMembershipLevel());
}
```

Setting `householdSize` on each *existing* household member has nothing to do with "cap the new owner's membership level." It's smuggled in only so that `member.getMembershipLevel()` picks up the large-household points bonus. That makes the class a catch-all: it caps *and* re-derives/mutates state on records other than the one it was handed. Every other function in this package (`CountHouseholdMembers`, etc.) mutates only the owner-in-hand; this one reaches out and writes to sibling entities, which a maintainer will not expect from a step named `CapMembershipLevel`, and it does so under `govern: [ transaction ]`, so those writes persist. `householdSize` is explicitly owned by `CountHouseholdMembers` (which documents it as recomputed-per-read, not stored). Keep that responsibility there; `CapMembershipLevel` should only read levels and set the cap. If an existing member's level needs its household bonus reflected, derive it without mutating the member (or compute it through the existing household-size path), rather than overwriting their field inline.

**2. Third copy of the "iterate `findAll()` and filter by `Household.id`" loop.**

`CapMembershipLevel` re-implements the exact pattern already present in `CountHouseholdMembers.service` and `CheckHouseholdUnique.service`:

```java
for (Owner existing : ownerRepository.findAll()) {
    if (householdId.equals(Household.id(existing.getLastName(), existing.getPostcode()))) { ... }
}
```

This is now the third verbatim copy of household-membership collection. Given `Household` is already positioned as the "single source of truth for household identity," the member-collection loop belongs next to it (e.g. a `List<Owner> membersOf(owner, repository)` helper) so the three callers share one definition instead of drifting independently.

**3. Minor — persisted cap vs. otherwise fully-derived level.**

`membershipLevelCap` is persisted while everything else feeding `getMembershipLevel()` (points, household size) is recomputed per read; the adjacent `householdSize` is deliberately *not* persisted. Storing one input and deriving the rest is a legitimate reading of "set once at creation," but it creates a persisted/derived split in one small area that's worth a one-line rationale at the field or a deliberate confirmation that the snapshot (not live recomputation) is intended.

